#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :question/id #uuid "0b146642-914c-52ce-9939-86752e95dcd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11013" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac{2}{3},$ find the value of $\\tan (-x+7\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan{x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x+7\\pi)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66a1733a-d7bb-5c86-a154-9e54422053a7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873 :topic/math-academy-id 725 :topic/title "Even and Odd Functions"}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515807 :question-generator/id #uuid "ee2be17c-7ff2-5ace-8a40-875aced950b8" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186176058.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19df7d47-b87b-5e46-8360-7533c525ec2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266358} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2908b8-2ead-5a77-be9f-804bc07bb7fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266672} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccc1b1b-9a04-5bb8-a591-9f5cc4c7efc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :an